The Accord is a circle of champions raised during the Eddic Crusade to be the eyes and arms of the six heroes who led it, [[Divinity/Hierophant|Aurelia]] and her first companions. Its members served all six of their heroes' patrons at once and put the good of Edan above any one of them. The histories of [[Geography/Færrin/index|Færrin]] do not mention them. Driven out of the world after the war, the Accord fights on centuries later from a refuge in the [[Geography/Færrin/Infinite Horizon/Far Realms/index|Far Realms]].

### The Eddic Crusade

The Eddic Crusade was a holy war of some forty years, fought by six heroes blessed by the gods who rose from the common folk to lead a sacred host. It ran on three fronts: to the north against the lich-king of the Dead Empire of [[Geography/Færrin/Calarium|Calarium]], to the east against the pestilent horde of the fiendish Rot Mother, and to the west across the barren, cursed wastes of the Allmind, a sentient incantation. The holy war against the Warlords of [[Geography/Færrin/Calaria/index|Calaria]] was the first of its three wars. The heroes gave their army a new kind of power, drawn from divine blessing rather than sorcery, and it swept the land at the cost of millions of lives.

Most of the host were not fighters. Farmers, traders and fishermen answered the call of one of the six and traded tools for weapons, and numbers alone would not have won even one of the three wars. The six could grant power and lead the army into battle, but they could not stand at every front or know every soul, so they raised the Accord: a few thousand trusted champions to train and guide the host, unified under all six patrons.

Soldiers told stories of Accord champions moving through the ranks, their chimeric [[Divinity/Divine Raiment|raiments]] bathing everyone near them in ever-changing light, turning a battle by foresight or by might. Statues and murals of a handful of these amalgamate defenders holding off three armies at once are said to survive, hidden in the forgotten and forbidden parts of [[Geography/Færrin/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]].

### Exile

As the Crusade wound down, its enemies slain and their holds taken, the host turned its weapons back into tools and disbanded, and the six heroes went their separate ways to find lands of their own. Where every church now staked its own claim, the Accord's raiments, once a sign of unity, met with derision. They were called impure and a sign of fence-sitting, and worst of all they showed that a follower could belong to several gods at once, against the rifts growing between the churches. The newly crowned powers of the age, the panjandrum, would not tolerate it. They drove the Accord out of Edan and out of the world entirely, far enough along the horizon that the people of Færrin forgot them.

### The Far Realms

Near the end of the war the Accord's war machine, a great city on legs called the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/index|Providence]], went missing, last seen walking through planes ever further from home. Adrift and looking for a purpose, the Accord set out to find it. Centuries of travel and fighting, and the help of a mysterious threshold across the [[Phenomena/Firmament|firmament]], brought them to the Far Realms. The deal that let them pass the firmament allowed no return.

They never found the strider there. They found an endless gray waste, hostile to life, crowded with interlopers who had come from far beyond to pry the firmament open and take what lies inside, and they took up a new war against them on Færrin's behalf.

The Accord still fights. Its refuge in the waste, the First Bastion, houses the many descendants of the first champions, and its warriors and tacticians cross the gray wastes to strike at any who would intrude. Its armor, magic and people have changed over the centuries as it scavenged from its foes, but every member still wears a six-colour raiment with pride.
